<owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0035869">
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0110165"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050"/>
      <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0005929"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002162"/>
      <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_2759"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A region of the cilium between the basal body and proximal segment that is characterized by Y-shaped assemblages that connect axonemal microtubules to the ciliary membrane. The ciliary transition zone appears to function as a gate that controls ciliary membrane composition and separates the cytosol from the ciliary plasm.</obo:IAO_0000115>
  <oboI:created_by rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">bf</oboI:created_by>
  <oboI:creation_date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2011-05-25T03:09:58Z</oboI:creation_date>
  <oboI:hasExactSynonym rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">cilial transition zone</oboI:hasExactSynonym>
  <oboI:hasExactSynonym rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">cilium transition zone</oboI:hasExactSynonym>
  <oboI:hasOBONamespace rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">cellular_component</oboI:hasOBONamespace>
  <oboI:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">connecting cilium</oboI:hasRelatedSynonym>
  <oboI:id rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GO:0035869</oboI:id>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Depending on the species, this region may have a distinct geometrically shaped electron-dense structure within the axonemal lumen visible in electron microscopy images; most animals don't display this inner structure. The axoneme extends through the ciliary transition zone, but only consists of the outer doublets. The central pair, axonemal spokes, and dynein complexes are not found in this part of the ciliary shaft. Note that the connecting cilium of the photoreceptor cells is thought to be equivalent to the transition zone.</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ciliary transition zone</rdfs:label>
</owl:Class>

